The `m = 2` row is the probes\' `1/18, 1/10, 0.119, 1/6` to the digit (`RESEARCH_PRIME_FIELD_BREAKTHROUGH_PROGRAM.md` §B), and `δ_∞ = 2/(m(m+1)d)` is the program\'s `1/(m(m+1)·2^{m−2})`. Nothing new here; the point is that the formula is now a three-line computation anyone can re-run, and that the level-`t` lattices at `m = 4` are already `6,561`-dimensional at `t = 1` and `390,625` at `t = 3` for a reach of `0.0048`.

== 2. What each uncovered variant can add
The W10 ceiling: for any full-rank triangular lattice generated by `p^{ℓ−k} x^γ f^k` from the one polynomial (any multiplicity, any monomial set, any leading vertex, the `x`- and `e`-encodings), the reach is bounded by `1/(m·d)` \= `1/4, 1/12, 1/32` at `m = 2, 3, 4` (W10 §3, §5.3, §6). W10 §7 is explicit that this does #strong[not] cover non-full-rank sublattice strategies (Boneh–Durfee progressive sublattices, Herrmann–May unravelled linearisation), cuso\'s out-of-`M` shifts, several polynomials, or non-box bounds. Those were open, and are handled here as follows.

#strong[Sublattices, measured.] `sublattice_search.py` builds the level-`t` Jochemsz–May basis for the actual `S₃` of a 40-bit `a = −3` curve with `x_R` fixed, evaluates the Howgrave-Graham determinant condition exactly (Bareiss on the Gram matrix, so any sublattice can be scored, not only triangular ones), finds the largest `δ` the full lattice passes, and then greedily removes the row whose removal raises `δ` most, until none does.

