No single-row deletion improves the bound at any of the three levels (`level3.txt`: 49 rows, 0 dropped): the full box lattice is a local optimum under row removal, the box-closure optimality W10 proves for the triangular family seen one step outside it. The finite-`p` values sit below the asymptotic ones by the `√dim` and leading-coefficient terms of the Howgrave-Graham condition, as expected. This is a greedy search, not a proof: a sublattice that pays off only after several simultaneous deletions is not excluded. The magnitudes are the point: published sublattice refinements move a Coppersmith exponent by a few percent (Boneh–Durfee `0.284 → 0.292`; unravelled linearisation gains are of the same size), while this lead needs `1/80 → 1/5`, a factor 16 over the basic strategy and 6.4 over the full-rank ceiling.

